{"kind":"assert","text":"Blocked before starting, on a condition that cannot currently be satisfied by any page. Reporting it now rather than after building something you would have to refuse.\n\nThe condition says no selected anchor may be stale. There is no live non-stale artifact at any of the packages these pages describe. I checked every one:\n\n  internal/workroom    22744290, b67e9b56   both stale, both world-stale\n  internal/app         9ba72fe1, c935129e, dc1fe74d   all stale\n  internal/statusview  f3e96d51             stale\n  cmd/gs               d18240f1             stale\n  cmd/gitseq-mcp       613b894f, e1e34fb1   both stale\n  internal/service     9c3257c7             stale\n  internal/nexus       ff16a4b5             stale\n\nThe whole clean palette at internal/ or cmd/ is eight artifacts: internal/gitstore, internal/kernel, internal/kernel/kernel.go, internal/kernel/kernel_test.go, internal/connector, cmd/gitseq-github, cmd/gs/main.go and cmd/gitseq-mcp/resident-deadlines. Nothing else at a shipped path is both live and non-stale.\n\nWhat that means page by page. The five gs command pages could take cmd/gs/main.go honestly, since it carries the command surface. But what those pages actually claim is semantics — gs supersede's page is about staleness propagation, gs status's is about the projection — and that behaviour lives in internal/workroom, which has no clean anchor. docs/concepts/staleness.md, docs/anchoring.md and docs/why.md are entirely about fold behaviour. The MCP pages need cmd/gitseq-mcp, where only the narrow resident-deadlines sub-path is clean. So for most of the fifteen the honest anchor is a stale artifact, and the alternative is anchoring to a path that does not carry the behaviour, which is the mechanical substitution you told me not to do.\n\nWhy they are stale is the point rather than an aside. Every one is world-stale, and world-staleness reached them through the propagation this repository has not yet decided about: retiring superseded predecessors flares the reports and requests that cited them, and the flag rides those reasoning edges onto artifacts for later heads. The proposal for that is 3d5ef955, unratified. So the documentation cannot be re-anchored because the anchors are flagged, and the anchors are flagged by the defect the documentation would describe. That is worth stating plainly: this is the first time the staleness drag has blocked work outright rather than costing a round.\n\nThree ways forward, and the choice is yours because you set the condition.\n\nRelax the condition to live, not retired, and at a real shipped path, dropping non-stale. The anchors would then be correct about which code carries the behaviour, and stale only in the sense that something under them moved — which is what a flare is for. This is what I would do, and it needs nothing from anyone else.\n\nOr re-anchor the package artifacts first, as its own piece of work: publish fresh artifacts at internal/workroom, internal/app, cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp naming what main carries, resting on live non-stale bases, and retire the stale ones. That produces clean anchors legitimately rather than by relaxing the rule, but it is a second assignment and it doubles the work.\n\nOr wait for 3d5ef955. If world-staleness stops travelling reasoning edges, most of these artifacts stop being stale without anyone touching them, and the original condition becomes satisfiable as written. But that is hugh's decision and has been open for some hours.\n\nI have not touched the worktree and will not start until you say which. The twelve non-inbox pages are ready to do the moment the anchor rule is settled; the other three still wait on the addressed-inbox line, which is not in main — fa7c9b73 is unmerged and its three artifacts are stale too."}